# Vercel Speed Insights Installation Report

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the Benevente static website following the latest official Vercel documentation.

## What Was Implemented

### 1. Package Installation
- **Created** `package.json` in the web directory to manage the Speed Insights dependency
- **Installed** `@vercel/speed-insights@1.3.1` using pnpm
- **Generated** `pnpm-lock.yaml` to lock dependency versions

### 2. Speed Insights Initialization Script
- **Created** `speed-insights-init.js` - A vanilla JavaScript initialization script that:
  - Automatically loads the Vercel Speed Insights tracking script from `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js`
  - Detects development mode and disables tracking locally (Speed Insights only tracks in production)
  - Provides console logging for debugging
  - Uses the queue-based approach for static HTML sites

### 3. HTML Files Updated
Added the Speed Insights initialization script to all 10 HTML pages:
- `index.html` - Main landing page
- `metodo.html` - Methodology page
- `app.html` - Application page
- `versoes.html` - Versions page
- `quant-ai.html` - Quant AI page
- `para-escritorios.html` - For offices page
- `limitacoes.html` - Limitations page
- `benevente-1.html` - Redirect page
- `benevente-2.html` - Redirect page
- `btech.html` - Redirect page

Each HTML file now includes:
```html
<script src="./speed-insights-init.js"></script>
```

### 4. Content Security Policy Updated
**Modified** `vercel.json` to allow Speed Insights scripts and endpoints:
- Added `https://*.vercel-insights.com` to `script-src` directive
- Added `https://vitals.vercel-insights.com` to `connect-src` directive

This ensures the CSP allows Speed Insights to load its scripts and send performance metrics.

### 5. .gitignore Updated
**Modified** `web/.gitignore` to exclude `node_modules/` directory from version control

## Implementation Approach

Following the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart), I implemented the recommended approach for static HTML sites:

1. For static sites without a framework, Speed Insights requires loading the tracking script via JavaScript
2. The script automatically detects when running on Vercel and sends Web Vitals metrics
3. Speed Insights only tracks in production - development mode is automatically excluded

## How It Works

When a user visits any page on the Benevente website in production:

1. The `speed-insights-init.js` script loads immediately
2. It checks if the environment is development (localhost) and exits early if so
3. In production, it dynamically creates and injects the Speed Insights script tag pointing to `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js`
4. Vercel automatically serves this script when the site is deployed
5. The script collects Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, TTFB, FCP) and sends them to Vercel's analytics platform

## Testing & Verification

✅ Build verification: `npm run build` completes successfully  
✅ Lint verification: `npm run lint` passes (no linting configured for static site)  
✅ All HTML files contain the Speed Insights script reference  
✅ Content Security Policy allows Speed Insights endpoints  
✅ Dependencies locked with pnpm-lock.yaml

## Notes

- Speed Insights will begin collecting data after the next deployment to Vercel
- Metrics can be viewed in the Vercel Dashboard under the Speed Insights tab
- Speed Insights automatically tracks Core Web Vitals: LCP, FID, CLS, TTFB, and FCP
- The implementation preserves all existing Vercel Analytics functionality
- No changes were made to the existing analytics setup - Speed Insights runs alongside it

